.TH ltr_udp 1 "21 July 2026"
.IX ltr_udp
.SH NAME
ltr_udp - send LinuxTrack pose data to an OpenTrack or FreeTrack UDP receiver.
.SH SYNOPSYS
.B ltr_udp
.RI [--proto=freetrack]
.RI [--ip=HOST]
.RI [--port=PORT]
.RI [--profile=PROFILE]
.RI [PORT]
.SH DESCRIPTION
ltr_udp is a LinuxTrack client executable that sends head tracking pose data
over UDP to a local or remote receiver.

By default, ltr_udp sends OpenTrack-compatible UDP packets to
.B 127.0.0.1:4242.

The default OpenTrack packet consists of six little-endian double precision
floating point values in the following order:
.BR X ,
.BR Y ,
.BR Z ,
.BR Yaw ,
.BR Pitch ,
.BR Roll .
Translation values are sent in centimeters. Rotation values are sent in
degrees.

ltr_udp can also send an alternative FreeTrack-style UDP packet when requested.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B Network options:
.TP
.B --ip=HOST
Destination IPv4 address (default: 127.0.0.1).
.TP
.B --port=PORT
Destination UDP port (default: 4242).
.TP
.B PORT
If an argument is not recognized as an option and is a number greater than 1024,
it is treated as the destination UDP port.
.TP
.B Protocol options:
.TP
.B --proto=freetrack
Send FreeTrack-style UDP packets instead of the default OpenTrack packet format.
.TP
.B LinuxTrack options:
.TP
.B --profile=PROFILE
LinuxTrack profile name. If omitted, the default LinuxTrack profile is used.
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
Send OpenTrack-compatible UDP data to the default receiver:
.B ltr_udp
.TP
Send OpenTrack-compatible UDP data to another port:
.B ltr_udp --port=4243
.TP
Equivalent shorthand using a positional port:
.B ltr_udp 4243
.TP
Send UDP data to another host:
.B ltr_udp --ip=192.168.1.50 --port=4242
.TP
Use a specific LinuxTrack profile:
.B ltr_udp --profile=MyProfile1
.TP
Send FreeTrack-style UDP packets:
.B ltr_udp --proto=freetrack
.SH SIGNALS
.TP
.B SIGINT
Stop ltr_udp and shut down LinuxTrack cleanly. This is normally sent by pressing
Ctrl+C.
.TP
.B SIGTERM
Stop ltr_udp and shut down LinuxTrack cleanly.
.SH NOTES
ltr_udp requires a working LinuxTrack setup. The LinuxTrack server must be
available and tracking must reach the RUNNING or PAUSED state before packets are
sent.

If tracking does not start, check that the LinuxTrack server is running and that
the selected profile is valid.